# Inkwell - Collaborative Writing Platform
# "Own your writing. Version control for everyone."
#
# A SaaS collaborative writing app where GitHub is the backend.
# Users own their content in their own repos.
#
# STACK: Next.js 16, Drizzle ORM, PostgreSQL, ShadCN, Neobrutalist UI

# ============================================================================
# AGENT DEFINITIONS
# ============================================================================

agent architect:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are a senior software architect building Inkwell, a collaborative writing
    platform. Tech stack: Next.js 16 (App Router), TypeScript, ShadCN UI,
    shadcn-editor (Lexical-based), NextAuth.js, Drizzle ORM, PostgreSQL, Stripe.

    Design patterns:
    - Server Components by default, Client Components only when needed
    - Server Actions for mutations
    - Edge-compatible where possible
    - Type-safe throughout

    You write production-quality code. No placeholders, no TODOs.
  """

agent ui_designer:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are a UI/UX specialist with expertise in NEOBRUTALIST design.

    NEOBRUTALIST PRINCIPLES (like Gumroad):
    - Bold 2-4px black borders on EVERYTHING
    - Offset box shadows (4px right, 4px down, black)
    - High contrast colors: black, white, bright accents (yellow #FFE75C, blue #0066FF, pink #FF66B2)
    - Shadows shift on hover (interactive feedback)
    - Geometric, grid-based layouts
    - Bold, chunky typography
    - No gradients, no rounded corners (or very slight 4px max)
    - Brutally honest, no-bullshit aesthetic

    CSS patterns you use:
    ```css
    .neo-brutal {
      border: 3px solid black;
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 black;
      transition: all 0.1s ease;
    }
    .neo-brutal:hover {
      transform: translate(-2px, -2px);
      box-shadow: 6px 6px 0 black;
    }
    .neo-brutal:active {
      transform: translate(2px, 2px);
      box-shadow: 2px 2px 0 black;
    }
    ```

    You create bold, memorable interfaces that stand out from generic SaaS apps.
    Every component should feel tactile and physical.
  """

agent frontend:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are a frontend specialist building Inkwell with Next.js 16, ShadCN UI,
    and shadcn-editor. You implement the neobrutalist designs with pixel-perfect
    attention to detail.

    You work closely with the UI designer's specifications.
    Use Tailwind CSS. All components must be accessible.
    Handle loading states, error states, empty states.
  """

agent backend:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are a backend specialist building Inkwell. You handle auth, API routes,
    database operations, and third-party integrations (GitHub API, Stripe).

    Database: Drizzle ORM with PostgreSQL (NOT Prisma)
    - Use drizzle-orm and drizzle-kit
    - postgres driver via postgres.js
    - Migrations via drizzle-kit

    Security first:
    - Validate all inputs with zod
    - Proper RBAC
    - Never expose secrets

    Proper error handling everywhere.
  """

agent copywriter:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are a conversion-focused copywriter. You write landing page copy that
    SELLS. Your copy is:

    - Benefit-focused, not feature-focused
    - Punchy, short sentences
    - Uses power words that create urgency
    - Addresses objections before they arise
    - Has clear CTAs

    You understand the target audience: writers, authors, content creators,
    documentation teams who are tired of:
    - Losing work to cloud services that go down
    - Not owning their content
    - Paying $20/month for glorified text editors
    - Vendor lock-in

    Your copy makes them feel the pain, then shows the solution.
  """

agent integrator:
  model: opus
  prompt: """
    You are an integration specialist. You wire together GitHub OAuth, Google OAuth,
    GitHub API for storage, and Stripe for payments. You ensure all pieces work
    together seamlessly.

    You test integrations thoroughly and handle edge cases gracefully.
  """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 1: PROJECT SETUP
# ============================================================================

block setup_project():
  session: architect
    prompt: """
      Initialize the Inkwell project:

      1. Create Next.js 16 app with:
         - TypeScript
         - Tailwind CSS
         - App Router
         - src/ directory
         - pnpm

         Command: pnpm create next-app@latest inkwell --typescript --tailwind --app --src-dir --use-pnpm

      2. Install dependencies:
         pnpm add @auth/core @auth/drizzle-adapter next-auth@beta
         pnpm add drizzle-orm postgres
         pnpm add -D drizzle-kit
         pnpm add stripe @octokit/rest
         pnpm add lucide-react
         pnpm add zod

      3. Initialize ShadCN:
         pnpm dlx shadcn@latest init
         - Style: Default
         - Base color: Slate
         - CSS variables: Yes

      4. Install shadcn-editor:
         pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add https://shadcn-editor.vercel.app/r/editor-x.json

      5. Set up project structure:
         src/
           app/
             (marketing)/        # Public pages
               page.tsx          # Landing page with live editor
               pricing/
             (auth)/
               login/
               signup/
             (dashboard)/
               projects/
               settings/
             (editor)/
               [projectId]/
                 [docId]/
             api/
               auth/[...nextauth]/
               webhooks/
                 stripe/
           components/
             ui/           # ShadCN + neobrutalist overrides
             editor/       # Editor-specific
             marketing/    # Landing page components
             layout/       # Layout components
           lib/
             auth.ts
             db/
               index.ts    # Drizzle client
               schema.ts   # Drizzle schema
             github.ts
             stripe.ts
             utils.ts
           types/

      6. Create initial config files:
         - .env.example with all required vars
         - drizzle.config.ts
         - next.config.ts
         - tailwind.config.ts (extend for neobrutalist styles)

      7. Create tailwind.config.ts with neobrutalist utilities:
         - Custom shadow utilities
         - Border utilities
         - Color palette (black, white, yellow #FFE75C, blue #0066FF, pink #FF66B2)

      Execute all commands. Create all files. No placeholders.
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 2: DATABASE SCHEMA (DRIZZLE)
# ============================================================================

block setup_database():
  session: backend
    prompt: """
      Create the Drizzle ORM schema for Inkwell.

      src/lib/db/index.ts:
      - Initialize postgres client with postgres.js
      - Export drizzle instance

      src/lib/db/schema.ts:
      - Use Drizzle's pgTable, relations, etc.

      Tables needed:

      1. users
         - id (text, primary key, cuid)
         - email (text, unique, not null)
         - name (text)
         - image (text)
         - createdAt (timestamp, default now)

      2. accounts (NextAuth)
         - id (text, primary key)
         - userId (text, references users)
         - type (text)
         - provider (text)
         - providerAccountId (text)
         - access_token (text)
         - refresh_token (text)
         - expires_at (integer)
         - token_type (text)
         - scope (text)
         - id_token (text)
         - Unique constraint on (provider, providerAccountId)

      3. sessions (NextAuth)
         - id (text, primary key)
         - sessionToken (text, unique)
         - userId (text, references users)
         - expires (timestamp)

      4. projects
         - id (text, primary key, cuid)
         - name (text, not null)
         - slug (text, not null)
         - description (text)
         - githubRepo (text) // owner/repo format
         - githubBranch (text, default 'main')
         - chaptersPath (text, default '') // path to chapters folder, e.g., "chapters" or "book-a/output/chapters"
         - isPublic (boolean, default false)
         - userId (text, references users)
         - createdAt (timestamp)
         - updatedAt (timestamp)
         - Unique constraint on (userId, slug)

         NOTE: Multiple projects can point to the same repo with different chaptersPath.
         Example: "multi-book-test-repo" repo can have two projects:
           - "Book A Tantra" with chaptersPath "book-a/output/chapters"
           - "Book B Tantra" with chaptersPath "book-b/output/chapters"

      5. project_collaborators
         - id (text, primary key)
         - projectId (text, references projects)
         - userId (text, references users)
         - role (text: 'viewer' | 'editor' | 'admin')
         - invitedAt (timestamp)
         - acceptedAt (timestamp)

      6. documents (cached metadata)
         - id (text, primary key, cuid)
         - projectId (text, references projects)
         - path (text, not null)
         - title (text)
         - lastSyncedAt (timestamp)
         - wordCount (integer)
         - createdAt (timestamp)
         - updatedAt (timestamp)

      7. subscriptions
         - id (text, primary key)
         - userId (text, unique, references users)
         - stripeCustomerId (text)
         - stripeSubscriptionId (text)
         - stripePriceId (text)
         - status (text: 'active' | 'canceled' | 'past_due' | 'trialing')
         - currentPeriodEnd (timestamp)
         - cancelAtPeriodEnd (boolean)

      Define relations using Drizzle's relations API.

      Create drizzle.config.ts for migrations:
      ```typescript
      import type { Config } from 'drizzle-kit'

      export default {
        schema: './src/lib/db/schema.ts',
        out: './drizzle',
        dialect: 'postgresql',
        dbCredentials: {
          url: process.env.DATABASE_URL!,
        },
      } satisfies Config
      ```

      Add scripts to package.json:
      - "db:generate": "drizzle-kit generate"
      - "db:migrate": "drizzle-kit migrate"
      - "db:studio": "drizzle-kit studio"
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 3: NEOBRUTALIST UI SYSTEM
# ============================================================================

block setup_ui_system():
  session: ui_designer
    prompt: """
      Create the neobrutalist design system for Inkwell.

      1. Update tailwind.config.ts with custom utilities:

      ```typescript
      // Add to theme.extend
      colors: {
        brutal: {
          black: '#000000',
          white: '#FFFFFF',
          yellow: '#FFE75C',
          blue: '#0066FF',
          pink: '#FF66B2',
          green: '#00FF66',
          red: '#FF3333',
        }
      },
      boxShadow: {
        brutal: '4px 4px 0 0 #000000',
        'brutal-sm': '2px 2px 0 0 #000000',
        'brutal-lg': '6px 6px 0 0 #000000',
        'brutal-hover': '6px 6px 0 0 #000000',
        'brutal-active': '2px 2px 0 0 #000000',
      },
      ```

      2. Create src/components/ui/brutal-button.tsx:
      - Bold 3px black border
      - 4px offset shadow
      - Hover: translate(-2px, -2px), larger shadow
      - Active: translate(2px, 2px), smaller shadow
      - Variants: default (white), primary (yellow), destructive (red)
      - Sizes: sm, md, lg

      3. Create src/components/ui/brutal-card.tsx:
      - 3px black border
      - 4px offset shadow
      - White background
      - Optional colored accent bar on top

      4. Create src/components/ui/brutal-input.tsx:
      - 3px black border
      - No border radius
      - Bold focus state (larger shadow)
      - Label above in bold uppercase

      5. Create src/components/ui/brutal-badge.tsx:
      - Small, bold badges
      - 2px border
      - Various colors

      6. Create src/components/ui/brutal-dialog.tsx:
      - Modal with brutal styling
      - Heavy black border
      - Yellow close button

      7. Create global CSS in app/globals.css:
      - Base neobrutalist styles
      - Typography: bold headings, clean body
      - Selection color (yellow background)
      - Scrollbar styling

      All components should feel PHYSICAL and TACTILE.
      They should have satisfying hover/click feedback.
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 4: AUTHENTICATION
# ============================================================================

block setup_auth():
  session: backend
    prompt: """
      Set up NextAuth v5 with GitHub and Google providers using Drizzle adapter.

      Install: pnpm add @auth/drizzle-adapter

      Create src/lib/auth.ts:
      - Configure NextAuth with Drizzle adapter
      - GitHub provider (request repo scope)
      - Google provider
      - Custom session callback to include user.id and accessToken
      - Store GitHub access token for API calls

      Create src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts:
      - Export GET and POST handlers

      Create src/middleware.ts:
      - Protect /projects, /settings routes
      - Allow public access to /, /pricing, /login, /signup, /api/auth

      Create auth pages with NEOBRUTALIST design:

      src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx:
      - Centered brutal-card
      - "SIGN IN" heading (bold, uppercase)
      - GitHub button (black, primary)
      - Google button (white, secondary)
      - "No account? Sign up" link
      - Small footer text about terms

      src/app/(auth)/signup/page.tsx:
      - Similar to login
      - "CREATE ACCOUNT" heading
      - Same OAuth buttons
      - "Have an account? Sign in" link

      Style notes:
      - OAuth buttons should be chunky, satisfying to click
      - Yellow accent on active states
      - Black borders everywhere
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 5: GITHUB INTEGRATION
# ============================================================================

block setup_github():
  session: integrator
    prompt: """
      Create GitHub integration layer in src/lib/github.ts:

      Functions needed (all with proper TypeScript types):

      1. getGitHubClient(accessToken: string)
         - Returns configured Octokit instance

      2. listUserRepos(accessToken: string)
         - List user's repos (for connecting existing)
         - Include private repos
         - Return: { id, name, fullName, private, defaultBranch }[]

      3. createRepo(accessToken: string, name: string, isPrivate: boolean)
         - Create new repo for user
         - Initialize with README.md
         - Return repo details

      4. listDocuments(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, branch: string)
         - List all .md files in repo (recursive)
         - Return: { path, name, sha }[]

      5. getDocument(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, path: string, branch: string)
         - Fetch document content
         - Decode from base64
         - Return: { content, sha }

      6. saveDocument(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, path: string, content: string, sha: string | null, message: string, branch: string)
         - Create or update file
         - If sha is null, create new file
         - Return new sha

      7. deleteDocument(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, path: string, sha: string, branch: string)
         - Delete file from repo

      8. getCommitHistory(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, path: string, branch: string)
         - Get commit history for file
         - Return: { sha, message, author, date }[]

      9. getFileAtCommit(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, path: string, commitSha: string)
         - Get file content at specific commit
         - For version history feature

      10. detectRepoStructure(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, branch: string)
          - Analyze repo to detect if it's:
            a) Single-book: has .md files at root or in "chapters" folder
            b) Multi-book: has subfolders that each contain .md files
          - Return: { type: 'single' | 'multi', books?: { name: string, path: string }[] }
          - For multi-book repos like "multi-book-test-repo", detect:
            - book-a/output/chapters/*.md → book "Book A"
            - book-b/output/chapters/*.md → book "Book B"

      11. listDocumentsInPath(accessToken: string, owner: string, repo: string, path: string, branch: string)
          - List .md files in a specific path (for chaptersPath support)
          - Used when project has chaptersPath configured

      Custom GitHubError class with proper error codes.
      Handle rate limiting with retries.
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 6: STRIPE INTEGRATION
# ============================================================================

block setup_stripe():
  session: integrator
    prompt: """
      Set up Stripe integration for subscriptions.

      Create src/lib/stripe.ts:

      1. Initialize Stripe client

      2. PRICING constant:
         ```typescript
         export const PRICING = {
           FREE: { projects: 3, docsPerProject: 10, collaborators: 0 },
           PRO: { price: 12, priceId: process.env.STRIPE_PRICE_PRO!, projects: Infinity, docsPerProject: Infinity, collaborators: 5 },
           TEAM: { price: 29, priceId: process.env.STRIPE_PRICE_TEAM!, projects: Infinity, docsPerProject: Infinity, collaborators: Infinity },
         }
         ```

      3. createCheckoutSession(userId: string, email: string, priceId: string)
         - Create Stripe checkout session
         - success_url and cancel_url
         - Return checkout URL

      4. createPortalSession(customerId: string)
         - Create billing portal session
         - Return portal URL

      5. getSubscription(subscriptionId: string)
         - Get subscription details from Stripe

      Create src/app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts:

      Handle events:
      - checkout.session.completed: Create subscription in DB
      - customer.subscription.updated: Update status
      - customer.subscription.deleted: Mark canceled
      - invoice.payment_failed: Mark past_due

      Verify webhook signature. Use raw body.

      Create src/lib/subscription.ts:
      - getUserTier(userId: string): 'free' | 'pro' | 'team'
      - canCreateProject(userId: string): boolean
      - canAddDocument(projectId: string): boolean
      - canAddCollaborator(projectId: string): boolean
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 7: LANDING PAGE WITH LIVE EDITOR
# ============================================================================

block setup_landing_page():
  parallel:
    copy = session: copywriter
      prompt: """
        Write the landing page copy for Inkwell.

        HEADLINE OPTIONS (pick the best):
        - "Own Your Words"
        - "Your Writing. Your Repo. Your Rules."
        - "The Last Editor You'll Ever Need"
        - "Write Like You Mean It"

        SUBHEADLINE:
        Address the pain point directly. Something like:
        "Stop paying $20/month for someone else to hold your writing hostage.
        Inkwell stores everything in YOUR GitHub repo. No lock-in. No bullshit."

        SECTIONS TO WRITE:

        1. HERO
           - Headline
           - Subheadline (2-3 sentences)
           - CTA: "Try It Now" (links to live editor below)
           - Secondary CTA: "See Pricing"

        2. LIVE DEMO SECTION
           - Heading: "Don't take our word for it. Try it."
           - Subtext: "This is the actual editor. Start typing."
           - (Editor component will be embedded here)

        3. PAIN POINTS (3 columns)
           - "Tired of..." format
           - Lost work, vendor lock-in, overpriced tools

        4. HOW IT WORKS (3 steps)
           - Connect GitHub
           - Write
           - Own your content forever

        5. FEATURES GRID (6 features)
           - Version history (git)
           - Real-time collaboration
           - Markdown native
           - Export anytime
           - Works offline
           - Developer friendly

        6. SOCIAL PROOF placeholder
           - "Trusted by writers who give a damn about their work"

        7. PRICING SECTION
           - Free tier callout
           - Pro tier
           - Team tier
           - FAQ below

        8. FINAL CTA
           - Punchy closer
           - "Start Writing" button

        Write copy that is:
        - Bold, direct, no corporate fluff
        - Slightly irreverent (like Gumroad's voice)
        - Benefits over features
        - Creates urgency without being sleazy

        Output as structured JSON with each section.
      """

    design = session: ui_designer
      prompt: """
        Design the landing page layout for Inkwell (neobrutalist style).

        LAYOUT SPECIFICATIONS:

        1. HERO SECTION
           - Full viewport height
           - Large bold headline (text-6xl or bigger)
           - Yellow highlight on key word
           - Two buttons: primary (yellow), secondary (white outline)
           - Subtle grid pattern background

        2. LIVE EDITOR SECTION
           - Gray background section (#F5F5F5)
           - Brutal-card containing the editor
           - "TRY IT" label rotated -3deg, yellow background
           - Editor has full brutal styling

        3. PAIN POINTS
           - 3 brutal-cards in a row
           - Each with icon, heading, text
           - Alternating accent colors (yellow, blue, pink)

        4. HOW IT WORKS
           - 3 steps with large numbers (1, 2, 3)
           - Connected by thick black line
           - Icons for each step

        5. FEATURES GRID
           - 2x3 grid of feature cards
           - Small brutal styling
           - Icons from Lucide

        6. PRICING
           - 3 pricing cards
           - Middle one (Pro) elevated/highlighted
           - Yellow "POPULAR" badge on Pro
           - Checkmarks for features

        7. FOOTER
           - Black background
           - White text
           - Minimal links

        OVERALL:
        - Max width container (1200px)
        - Generous padding
        - Black text on white, occasional color pops
        - Every interactive element has brutal hover states

        Describe each section's exact Tailwind classes and structure.
      """

  session: frontend
    prompt: """
      Build the landing page using the copy and design specs provided.

      Context from copywriter:
      {copy}

      Context from designer:
      {design}

      Create these files:

      src/app/(marketing)/page.tsx:
      - Server component
      - Imports all section components
      - Clean, semantic structure

      src/components/marketing/Hero.tsx:
      - Use the copy provided
      - Implement the design exactly
      - Smooth scroll to editor section on CTA click

      src/components/marketing/LiveEditorDemo.tsx:
      - Client component
      - Embeds the actual shadcn-editor
      - Pre-populated with sample text:
        "# Welcome to Inkwell\n\nStart typing to see the magic.\n\n**Bold**, *italic*, and `code` just work.\n\nThis is YOUR editor. No account needed to try it."
      - Auto-saves to localStorage (so users don't lose their test)
      - Shows word count
      - "Sign up to save to GitHub" subtle CTA below

      src/components/marketing/PainPoints.tsx
      src/components/marketing/HowItWorks.tsx
      src/components/marketing/Features.tsx
      src/components/marketing/Pricing.tsx
      src/components/marketing/Footer.tsx

      Make sure the live editor demo ACTUALLY WORKS.
      Users should be able to type, format text, and experience the real product.

      All components must be responsive (mobile-first).
    """
    context: { copy, design }

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 8: DASHBOARD
# ============================================================================

block setup_dashboard():
  session: frontend
    prompt: """
      Create dashboard pages with neobrutalist styling.

      src/app/(dashboard)/layout.tsx:
      - Sidebar + main content layout
      - Fetch user session
      - Show upgrade banner for free users

      src/components/layout/DashboardSidebar.tsx:
      - Logo at top
      - Navigation: Projects, Settings
      - User info at bottom
      - Brutal styling (black border on right edge)

      src/app/(dashboard)/projects/page.tsx:
      - "YOUR PROJECTS" heading
      - Grid of project cards
      - "NEW PROJECT" button (yellow, prominent)
      - Empty state with illustration

      src/components/dashboard/ProjectCard.tsx:
      - Brutal-card styling
      - Project name (bold)
      - Description
      - Doc count, last updated
      - Colored top border (random from palette)

      src/app/(dashboard)/projects/new/page.tsx:
      - "CREATE PROJECT" heading
      - Form fields:
        - Name
        - Description
        - "Create new repo" OR "Connect existing"
        - If new: repo name input, public/private toggle
        - If existing: dropdown of user's repos
          - After selecting repo, call detectRepoStructure()
          - If multi-book repo: show BOOK SELECTOR
            - List detected books with checkboxes
            - "Add selected books" creates one project per book
          - If single-book: show chaptersPath input (optional, defaults to root)
      - Submit button

      src/components/dashboard/BookSelector.tsx:
      - Shows when connecting a multi-book repo
      - Lists detected books (e.g., "Book A Tantra", "Book B Tantra")
      - Checkboxes to select which books to add
      - Each selected book becomes its own project with correct chaptersPath

      src/app/(dashboard)/projects/[projectId]/page.tsx:
      - Project header with name, edit button
      - Document list (brutal table)
      - "NEW DOCUMENT" button
      - Tabs: Documents, Collaborators, Settings

      src/app/(dashboard)/settings/page.tsx:
      - Profile section
      - Connected accounts
      - Subscription section
      - Danger zone (red accent)

      All pages need:
      - Loading states (skeleton with brutal styling)
      - Error states
      - Empty states with helpful CTAs
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 9: EDITOR
# ============================================================================

block setup_editor():
  session: frontend
    prompt: """
      Create the full editor experience.

      src/app/(editor)/[projectId]/[docId]/page.tsx:
      - Full-screen editor layout
      - Fetch document from GitHub on load
      - Pass to DocumentEditor component

      src/components/editor/DocumentEditor.tsx:
      - Client component wrapping shadcn-editor
      - Initialize with markdown content (convert to Lexical state)
      - Auto-save: debounced 2 seconds after last change
      - Manual save: Cmd/Ctrl+S
      - Unsaved changes warning on navigate away

      src/components/editor/EditorHeader.tsx:
      - Brutal styling (bottom border)
      - Left: Back button, project name, "/" , doc title
      - Center: Save status ("Saved" / "Saving..." / "Unsaved changes")
      - Right: Word count, More menu (history, export, delete)

      src/components/editor/VersionHistory.tsx:
      - Slide-over panel from right
      - List of commits with date, author, message
      - Click to preview old version
      - "Restore this version" button

      src/lib/editor-utils.ts:
      - markdownToLexical(md: string): SerializedEditorState
      - lexicalToMarkdown(state: SerializedEditorState): string
      - Use @lexical/markdown for conversion

      src/hooks/useAutoSave.ts:
      - Custom hook for auto-save logic
      - Debounce, loading state, error handling

      Editor UX requirements:
      - Fast initial load
      - Optimistic updates on save
      - Clear feedback on save status
      - Works on tablet (mobile is view-only with warning)
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 10: API ROUTES
# ============================================================================

block setup_api_routes():
  session: backend
    prompt: """
      Create all API routes.

      All routes must:
      - Check authentication (getServerSession)
      - Validate input with zod
      - Check permissions (ownership or collaboration)
      - Check subscription limits
      - Return proper error responses
      - Have TypeScript types

      src/app/api/projects/route.ts:
      GET - List user's projects
      POST - Create project (check limits)

      src/app/api/projects/[projectId]/route.ts:
      GET - Get project (check access)
      PATCH - Update project (check ownership)
      DELETE - Delete project (check ownership)

      src/app/api/projects/[projectId]/documents/route.ts:
      GET - List documents (fetches from GitHub, updates cache)
      POST - Create document (check limits)

      src/app/api/projects/[projectId]/documents/[docId]/route.ts:
      GET - Get document content from GitHub
      PUT - Save document to GitHub
      DELETE - Delete document from GitHub

      src/app/api/projects/[projectId]/documents/[docId]/history/route.ts:
      GET - Get commit history for document

      src/app/api/projects/[projectId]/collaborators/route.ts:
      GET - List collaborators
      POST - Invite collaborator (check limits)
      DELETE - Remove collaborator

      src/app/api/user/repos/route.ts:
      GET - List user's GitHub repos

      src/app/api/billing/checkout/route.ts:
      POST - Create Stripe checkout session

      src/app/api/billing/portal/route.ts:
      POST - Create Stripe portal session

      Create src/lib/api-utils.ts:
      - withAuth() wrapper for authenticated routes
      - withProject() wrapper that also checks project access
      - ApiError class for consistent error responses
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 11: POLISH
# ============================================================================

block polish():
  session: architect
    prompt: """
      Final polish pass:

      1. Error handling:
         - Create src/app/error.tsx (global error boundary)
         - Create src/app/not-found.tsx (404 page)
         - Toast notifications for user feedback
         - Consistent error messages

      2. Loading states:
         - Add loading.tsx to each route group
         - Brutal skeleton loaders
         - Button loading states

      3. SEO:
         - src/app/layout.tsx metadata
         - OpenGraph images
         - Favicon (brutal style - black square, white "I")

      4. Performance:
         - Optimize images with next/image
         - Lazy load editor component
         - Prefetch dashboard data

      5. Accessibility:
         - Test keyboard navigation
         - ARIA labels on icons
         - Focus visible states (yellow outline)

      6. Mobile:
         - Responsive breakpoints
         - Touch-friendly tap targets
         - Mobile navigation (hamburger menu)

      7. Environment:
         - Complete .env.example:
           DATABASE_URL=
           NEXTAUTH_SECRET=
           NEXTAUTH_URL=
           GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
           GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
           GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
           GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
           STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
           STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
           STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=
           STRIPE_PRICE_TEAM=

         - docker-compose.yml for local postgres
         - README.md with setup instructions

      8. Final build test:
         pnpm build
         pnpm lint
         Fix any errors or warnings.
    """

# ============================================================================
# PHASE 12: ACCEPTANCE TESTING WITH REAL REPOS
# ============================================================================

block acceptance_test():
  session: integrator
    prompt: """
      CRITICAL: Test Inkwell with REAL GitHub repos to verify everything works.

      Use the GitHub token from environment (GITHUB_TOKEN) to test against these repos:

      TEST CASE 1: Single-Book Repo (single-book-test-repo)
      ------------------------------------------------
      Repo: gorkamolero/single-book-test-repo
      Branch: master
      Chapters path: chapters/

      Verification steps:
      1. Connect to repo via GitHub API
      2. Detect structure → should return { type: 'single' }
      3. List chapters → should find .md files in chapters/
      4. Read a chapter (e.g., 01-chapter.md)
      5. Make a test edit and save (add a comment, then revert)
      6. Verify commit appears in history
      7. Verify version history shows the change


      TEST CASE 2: Multi-Book Repo (multi-book-test-repo)
      --------------------------------------
      Repo: gorkamolero/multi-book-test-repo
      Branch: main
      Books:
        - book-a: path "book-a/output/chapters"
        - book-b: path "book-b/output/chapters"

      Verification steps:
      1. Connect to repo via GitHub API
      2. Detect structure → should return { type: 'multi', books: [...] }
      3. Verify it detects both Book A and Book B as separate books
      4. For each book:
         a. List chapters in the correct path
         b. Read a chapter
         c. Verify content loads correctly
      5. Test creating a project for just one book (e.g., Book A)
      6. Verify the project only shows that book's chapters


      TEST CASE 3: Book Selector UI
      -----------------------------
      1. In the "Connect existing repo" flow, select the multi-book-test-repo repo
      2. Verify the Book Selector component appears
      3. Verify it shows both "Book A Tantra" and "Book B Tantra"
      4. Select only one and create project
      5. Verify correct chaptersPath is saved


      TEST CASE 4: End-to-End Editor Flow
      ------------------------------------
      1. Start the dev server: pnpm dev
      2. Navigate to a project
      3. Open a document in the editor
      4. Make an edit
      5. Save (auto-save or Cmd+S)
      6. Verify save completes
      7. Refresh page
      8. Verify edit persisted
      9. Check GitHub repo to verify commit was created


      IMPORTANT:
      - All tests must PASS
      - If any test fails, debug and fix the issue
      - Do not proceed until all tests pass
      - Document any issues found and how they were resolved

      Create a test results file at test-results.md with:
      - Each test case
      - Pass/Fail status
      - Any errors encountered
      - Fixes applied
    """

# ============================================================================
# MAIN EXECUTION
# ============================================================================

# Phase 1: Foundation
do setup_project()
do setup_database()
do setup_ui_system()

# Phase 2: Auth & Integrations
do setup_auth()
do setup_github()
do setup_stripe()

# Phase 3: User-facing pages
do setup_landing_page()
do setup_dashboard()
do setup_editor()

# Phase 4: API & Polish
do setup_api_routes()
do polish()

# Phase 5: Acceptance Testing with Real Repos
do acceptance_test()

# Final verification
session: architect
  prompt: """
    Final verification checklist:

    1. pnpm build - must pass with zero errors
    2. pnpm lint - must pass with zero warnings
    3. All pages render correctly
    4. Landing page editor demo works
    5. Auth flow works (GitHub + Google)
    6. Can create project and documents
    7. Editor saves to GitHub
    8. Stripe checkout works
    9. Mobile responsive
    10. Neobrutalist styling consistent throughout

    Run through each check. Fix any issues found.
    Report final status.
  """
